(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-infancy-adolescence-nutrition-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Nutrition from Infancy Through Adolescence Quiz (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"The lecture emphasizes that the brain grows faster during which period than at any other time?","choices":["The preschool years, ages 4 to 5","The first year of life","The teenage growth spurt","The toddler years, ages 2 to 3"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the brain grows faster during the first year than at any other time. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"On a standard growth chart, which percentile is considered average?","choices":["The 25th percentile","The 50th percentile","The 5th percentile","The 95th percentile"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the 50th percentile is considered average on a growth chart. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The lecture notes that among infants and children who fail to grow as expected, what share have no apparent underlying disease?","choices":["About 5%","About 30%","About 50%","About 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: about 80% of children with failure to thrive have no apparent disease, pointing to environmental factors. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"The lecture cites about how many kilocalories per day for a 6-month-old child, met by breast milk or formula?","choices":["About 1,500 kilocalories per day","About 700 kilocalories per day","About 200 kilocalories per day","About 2,000 kilocalories per day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: about 700 kilocalories per day is cited for a 6-month-old, supplied by breast milk or formula. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"The lecture lists warning signs of infant dehydration. Which set matches?","choices":["More than 6 hours without a wet diaper and absence of tears when crying","Frequent wet diapers and pale, dilute urine","Rapid weight gain and puffy limbs","Increased tearing and drooling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: signs include more than 6 hours without a wet diaper and no tears when crying, among others. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 168"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what share of the calories in breast milk is supplied by protein?","choices":["About 35% to 40%","About 75%","Less than 10%","About 55%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: protein supplies less than 10% of the total calories in breast milk. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 180"},{"q":"The lecture notes an interesting property of breast milk related to the mother's diet. What is it?","choices":["Flavors of the mother's diet are transferred into her milk","The milk's fat content drops to zero after weaning foods begin","The milk becomes allergenic to the infant over time","The milk loses all protein by 6 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: flavors of the mother's diet are transferred into her breast milk. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 180"},{"q":"The lecture states infant formulas are fortified with vitamins and minerals except for which one?","choices":["Fluoride","Iron","Calcium","Vitamin D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: formulas are fortified with vitamins and minerals except for fluoride. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 191"},{"q":"The lecture suggests burping an infant after roughly how much time or intake, since infants swallow air?","choices":["After about 10 minutes or 1 to 2 ounces consumed","Only at the very end of a full feeding","After every single swallow","Burping is unnecessary for infants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: burp after about 10 minutes or once 1 to 2 ounces are consumed. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 205"},{"q":"The lecture lists general readiness signs for solid foods. Which matches?","choices":["Reaching about 25 pounds and refusing all milk","Sleeping through the night for a week","Having a full set of molars","Reaching about 13 pounds and consuming more than 32 ounces of formula daily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: readiness signs include reaching about 13 pounds and taking more than 32 ounces of formula daily or breastfeeding 8 to 10 times in 24 hours. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 216"},{"q":"Which first solid food does the lecture specifically suggest introducing, and in what serving size at first?","choices":["Whole cow's milk, starting with 4 ounces","Honey, starting with 1 tablespoon","Mixed table foods, a full bowl at a time","Iron-fortified rice cereal, starting with a 1-teaspoon serving"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: iron-fortified cereals such as rice cereal are suggested, starting with a 1-teaspoon serving. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 243"},{"q":"When first spoon-feeding an infant, the lecture says to expect the infant to take about how much?","choices":["Nothing at all for the first month","Only 2 or 3 bites","A full jar of baby food","At least 1 cup of puree"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: expect an infant to take only 2 or 3 bites at first meals. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 255"},{"q":"According to the lecture, infants should be able to drink from a cup by what age and be weaned from the bottle by what age?","choices":["Drink from a cup by 3 months and be weaned by 6 months","Drink from a cup by 3 years and be weaned by 4 years","There is no recommended weaning age","Drink from a cup by 1 year and be weaned from the bottle by 18 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: infants should drink from a cup by 1 year and be weaned from the bottle by 18 months. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 268"},{"q":"The lecture identifies which nutrient as often consumed in excess by children, largely from fast foods and processed foods?","choices":["Fiber","Sodium","Iron","Calcium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: sodium is often consumed in excess, about 1,000 milligrams per day more than needed, from fast and processed foods. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 331"},{"q":"The lecture lists nutrition interventions sometimes used for children with autism spectrum disorder. Which matches?","choices":["Exclusive breast milk through age 5","A gluten-free, casein-free diet with supplements such as probiotics and omega-3 fatty acids","A high-sodium, high-sugar diet","Complete elimination of all protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: interventions listed include a gluten-free, casein-free diet and supplements such as probiotics, vitamin B6, magnesium, and omega-3 fatty acids. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 413"},{"q":"The lecture cites an American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation to screen children's cholesterol at which ages?","choices":["Between ages 9 and 11","At birth","Only after age 18","Between ages 1 and 2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture cites cholesterol screening between ages 9 and 11. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 456"},{"q":"According to the lecture, roughly what share of youth are overweight at the time they are diagnosed with type 2 diabetes?","choices":["About 40%","About 100%","About 85%","About 10%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: about 85% of youth are overweight at diagnosis of type 2 diabetes. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 470"},{"q":"The lecture states about what fraction of school children do not eat breakfast, missing nutrients that fuel the brain and body?","choices":["About 1 in 100","About 90%","Almost none","About one-third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: as many as one-third of school children do not eat breakfast, worsening into the teenage years. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 483"},{"q":"The lecture credits which 2010 law with setting new standards for foods served in schools?","choices":["The Older Americans Act","The Dietary Guidelines for Americans","The Healthy, Hunger-Free Kids Act","The Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the 2010 Healthy, Hunger-Free Kids Act set new standards for foods served in schools. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 500"},{"q":"During the adolescent growth spurt, the lecture notes girls and boys gain body tissue differently. Which is correct?","choices":["Girls gain fat and lean tissue, while boys gain mainly lean tissue","Both girls and boys gain mainly fat tissue","Girls gain only lean tissue and boys gain only fat","Neither gains any tissue during the spurt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: girls gain both fat and lean tissue, whereas boys gain mainly lean tissue. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 514"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how much calcium per day do 9-to-18-year-old boys and girls need?","choices":["1,300 milligrams per day","700 milligrams per day","500 milligrams per day","2,500 milligrams per day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: teenagers ages 9 to 18 need 1,300 milligrams of calcium per day. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 535"},{"q":"The lecture notes soft drinks replacing milk contributes to low intake of which nutrients among teenagers?","choices":["Iron and vitamin B12","Fiber and potassium","Calcium and vitamin D","Sodium and added sugar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: soft drinks replacing milk contribute to low calcium and vitamin D intake in teenagers. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 535"},{"q":"The lecture cites an American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation to limit caffeine for adolescents to what amount, if any?","choices":["1,000 milligrams per day","There is no recommended limit","100 milligrams per day, if at all","500 milligrams per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the recommendation is to limit caffeine to 100 milligrams per day, if any. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 562"},{"q":"The lecture states that on any given day, about what share of the nation's youth eat fast food?","choices":["About 10%","About 40%","About 5%","About 75%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: about 40% of the nation's youth eat fast food on any given day. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 553"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish a food allergy from a food intolerance?","choices":["A food allergy never involves the immune system","A food intolerance is always more severe than an allergy","The two terms mean exactly the same thing","A food allergy involves an immune response, whereas a food intolerance does not"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a food allergy involves an immune response, while a food intolerance is a reaction that does not involve an allergic (immune) mechanism. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 601"},{"q":"According to the lecture's definitions, how are allergens related to antigens?","choices":["Allergens and antigens are unrelated","Neither allergens nor antigens involve the immune system","All allergens are antigens, but not all antigens are allergens","All antigens are allergens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: allergens are a subset of antigens, so all allergens are antigens but not all antigens are allergens. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 611"},{"q":"The Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act cited in the lecture requires manufacturers to do what?","choices":["Test every consumer for food allergies","Ban the sale of peanuts and tree nuts","Clearly identify the presence of major food allergens on product labels","Remove all allergens from processed foods"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the act requires manufacturers to clearly identify the presence of major food allergens on labels. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 666"},{"q":"The lecture notes that introducing foods other than human milk or formula before what age is associated with a higher risk of allergic disease?","choices":["Before 6 weeks of age","Before 4 months of age","Before 1 year of age","Before 2 years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: introducing other foods before 4 months of age is associated with higher risk of allergic diseases. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 675"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what share of young children outgrow food allergies before 3 years of age?","choices":["About 10%","About 40%","Almost none outgrow them","About 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: about 80% of young children outgrow food allergies before 3 years of age. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 657"},{"q":"The lecture notes iron-deficiency anemia in teenagers may appear during growth spurts and that which group may need a multivitamin with iron?","choices":["Menstruating teenage girls","All teenage boys","Only infants under 6 months","Teenagers who avoid all dairy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture notes menstruating teenage girls may need a multivitamin with iron. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 544"}]};
